Advanced Logic for Polonium (Without Code)
AI as a Service - Strategic Logic
1. Intelligent Provider Routing
Route queries based on complexity and cost:

Simple queries (what does this function do?) → cheap local models

Complex reasoning (why is this architecture flawed?) → premium models (GPT-4/Claude)

Code generation → specialized code models

Embeddings → dedicated embedding models

Real-time chat → fastest available model

2. Hybrid Caching Strategy
Semantic caching: Store embeddings of queries, not exact matches

Time-aware invalidation: AI responses expire based on repository activity

Differential caching: Cache per-file analysis, recompute only changed portions

Predictive prefetching: Pre-cache files related to user's current exploration path

3. Cost Optimization Layer
Batch processing: Group similar file analysis requests

Compression: Quantize embeddings from 1536-dim to 128-dim with 95% accuracy retention

Tiered storage: Hot embeddings in RAM, warm in Qdrant, cold in Postgres

Query aggregation: Combine multiple questions about same file into one LLM call

Advanced Data Structure Logic
1. Multi-Layered Indexing Pyramid
Layer 1 - Symbol Index (Trie + Bloom)

Store all function/class/variable names in compressed tries

Bloom filters for rapid "does this symbol exist?" checks

Enable O(1) symbol lookup and O(k) prefix searches

Layer 2 - Structural Index (AST)

Abstract Syntax Tree for each file

Dependency graph connecting all symbols across files

Enables "find all callers" and "impact analysis" in milliseconds

Layer 3 - Semantic Index (Vector)

Embeddings for code meaning, not just structure

Enables "find code similar to this concept" queries

Hierarchical navigation (repo → module → class → function)

2. Change Detection Logic
Merkle Tree Comparison

Build cryptographic hash tree of entire repository

Compare root hashes to detect ANY change

Binary search through tree to find EXACTLY what changed

O(log n) change detection vs O(n) full scan

Differential Indexing

Only re-index files that changed

Update dependency graphs incrementally

Propagate changes through affected paths only

3. Smart Retrieval Logic
Two-Stage Retrieval

Stage 1: Structural search (AST + Trie) - millisecond response

Stage 2: Semantic search (Vectors) - 100ms response

Hybrid ranking combining both signals

Context-Aware Chunking

Don't split at arbitrary character counts

Split at logical boundaries (functions, classes, paragraphs)

Overlap chunks by 20% to maintain context

Keep import statements and docstrings with every chunk

Relevance Scoring

TF-IDF for keyword matches

Graph proximity for related symbols

Recency weighting for active files

User behavior signals (what they click/view)

4. Interval Tree for Line-Based Queries
Logic: When a user asks about line 42, quickly find:

Which function contains line 42?

Which class contains that function?

What imports are relevant to this scope?

All in O(log n + k) time

5. Lazy Loading & Progressive Disclosure
File Tree Logic

Only load current directory contents

Prefetch next level on hover

Cache expanded branches

Virtual scrolling for massive repos

Code Analysis Logic

Parse imports first (dependencies)

Parse function signatures next (interfaces)

Parse implementations only when clicked

Generate AI explanations on-demand

6. Smart Connection Strategies
WebSocket Management

One connection per repository room

Automatic reconnection with state sync

Message batching (group 10 messages into 1)

Compression for large payloads

Real-time Collaboration Logic

Operational transforms for chat messages

Presence indicators using heartbeat

Typing indicators with debouncing

Message history pagination (load 50 at a time)

Performance Optimization Logic
1. Parallel Processing Pipeline
Repository Ingestion

Phase 1 (Parallel): Fetch all file metadata from GitHub API

Phase 2 (Threaded): Download file contents (I/O bound)

Phase 3 (Process): Parse AST and extract symbols (CPU bound)

Phase 4 (Batch): Generate embeddings in groups of 50

Each phase streams to next without waiting for completion

2. Intelligent Prefetching
Predict what user will need next:

If browsing file explorer → Prefetch sibling directories

If viewing file → Prefetch imported modules

If asking questions → Prefetch related files from vector search

If viewing function → Prefetch its test file

3. Adaptive Quality
Based on system load and query complexity:

Low load: Use full AST + embeddings + GPT-4

Medium load: Use AST + cached embeddings + Claude

High load: Use trie search + local LLM + cached responses

Peak load: Serve from cache only, queue analysis tasks

4. Write-Optimized vs Read-Optimized
Write path (repository updates):

Use Merkle tree for change detection

Update bloom filters and tries

Queue re-embedding tasks

Eventually consistent (2-5 second lag)

Read path (user queries):

Use read replicas for all indexes

Cache hot paths aggressively

Serve stale data with staleness indicator

Background refresh

Memory Management Logic
1. Conversation Memory
Not just storing messages, but:

Extracting facts from discussions (user learned X about function Y)

Building relationship graph (function A depends on B according to team)

Tracking unresolved questions (requires follow-up)

Summarizing long threads into memory snapshots

2. Repository Memory
Explicit memory: User-saved insights (pin important findings)

Implicit memory: AI-learned patterns from discussions

Episodic memory: Past questions and their contexts

Semantic memory: Facts about the codebase

3. Memory Consolidation
Short-term: Last 50 messages, full detail

Medium-term: Last 24 hours, summarized

Long-term: Important patterns only, vectorized

Archival: Everything else, compressed, searchable

